      "b10_source": "DeForest's labor & Employment practice advises employers whose workforce stability is fundamental to business continuity. Acting as strategic counsel to multinational and domestic companies, the team combines labor advisory, litigation, collective labor relations, social security, compliance and workforce governance into a fully integrated national platform capable of supporting complex operations throughout Mexico.\nRather than approaching labor law as a reactive discipline, the practice helps employers anticipate labor risk, strengthen governance frameworks and preserve operational continuity throughout the entire employment lifecycle—from hiring and day-to-day advisory to collective bargaining, workforce restructurings, regulatory inspections, social security matters and high-value litigation.\nThe practice has developed particular strength advising companies operating in highly regulated and labor-intensive industries, including automotive, advanced manufacturing, infrastructure, energy, logistics, retail, technology, telecommunications and financial services, where employment strategy directly affects production continuity, investment certainty and long-term business performance.\nThe department is led by one of the country's most experienced labor teams. Eduardo Garduño has become one of Mexico's leading labor strategists for multinational employers, combining more than two decades of experience advising major corporations with senior public-sector insight that enables him to translate complex regulatory environments into practical business solutions.\nJaime Bustamante contributes the perspective of one of the region's largest employers, having previously served as Legal Director for Mexico, Central and South America at ManpowerGroup, where he oversaw workforce structures across multiple jurisdictions.\nRaymundo Carreño brings the institutional knowledge accumulated during nearly four decades as General Legal Director of Volkswagen de México, offering clients strategic judgement developed through some of the country's most significant labor negotiations and organisational transformations.\nThe leadership team is further strengthened by Javier Atzin Vallejo, whose practice focuses on labor compliance, social security and workforce integration; Edgar Barreto, recognised for his experience in labor litigation and IMSS matters; Andrés Cabrera, who leads the firm's labor practice across the Bajío region; Erick Pérez, a specialist in complex litigation and constitutional proceedings; and José Alberto Díaz, whose practice combines labor, social security and administrative litigation in highly regulated industries.\nToday the practice advises employers whose combined workforce exceeds one million employees throughout Mexico. Its work extends well beyond litigation management and encompasses nationwide litigation portfolios involving hundreds of simultaneous proceedings, including approximately 700 labor disputes managed by the firm's Puebla team. The practice also advises on collective bargaining negotiations, union representation disputes, labor integration following mergers and acquisitions, employer restructurings, labor and social security audits, constitutional litigation, preventive governance programmes and regulatory inspections before the Ministry of Labor, IMSS and INFONAVIT.\nClients consistently retain DeForest not simply to resolve labor disputes, but to preserve organisational stability in environments where workforce disruption could directly affect production, investment and corporate reputation. This preventive, business-oriented philosophy has positioned the practice among Mexico's leading advisers to multinational employers navigating increasingly sophisticated labor challenges.\nDuring the last twelve months alone, the team has also assisted more than twenty clients in connection with labor inspections conducted by the Ministry of labor and has handled numerous audits involving labor and social security compliance, reinforcing its position as trusted counsel for employers operating in highly regulated sectors.\nThe firm's sustained growth is reflected in its recognition by Chambers Latin America 2025 for General Business Law (Querétaro), The Legal 500 since 2024 for labor & Employment and IFLR1000 2025 for Corporate & M&A, confirming the continued consolidation of DeForest as one of Mexico's most sophisticated full-service business law firms.",
      "b10_draft": "DeForest’s Labour & Employment practice advises employers whose workforce stability is fundamental to business continuity. The team combines labour advisory, litigation, collective labour relations, social security, compliance and workforce governance for multinational and domestic companies operating throughout Mexico.\n\nThe practice supports the full employment lifecycle, from day-to-day advice and preventive documentation to collective bargaining, workforce restructurings, labour and social-security audits, inspections, constitutional proceedings and litigation portfolios. Its work is concentrated in labour-intensive and regulated sectors, including automotive and advanced manufacturing, infrastructure, energy, logistics, retail, technology, telecommunications and financial services.\n\nThe department is led by Eduardo Garduño, with Javier Atzin Vallejo and Jaime Bustamante as Partners. The wider team includes José Alberto Díaz Méndez, Andrés Cabrera Gómez, Raymundo Carreño, Edgar Barreto and Erick Pérez. Their combined experience supports collective labour strategy, compliance, social security, litigation and regional implementation.\n\nRepresentative work includes post-acquisition labour integration across six Mexican states for more than 5,000 employees; collective bargaining and union-representation matters involving strike-prevention strategy; nationwide litigation portfolios; workforce restructurings; and labour and social-security governance for industrial operations. The practice also manages approximately 700 labour disputes through its Puebla team and has assisted more than 20 clients in Ministry of Labor inspections during the last 12 months.",
      "c2_source": "",
      "c2_draft": "DeForest requests consideration for coverage in Labour & Employment in Mexico. The practice presents employer-side work across the areas most central to workforce continuity in labour-intensive operations: collective bargaining and union-representation disputes, post-acquisition integration, workforce restructuring, employment litigation, constitutional labour procedure, labour inspections, social security and preventive governance.\n\nThe team has implemented a post-acquisition labour integration across six Mexican states for more than 5,000 employees; designed collective bargaining and union-representation strategies that prevented work stoppages; coordinated nationwide litigation portfolios involving approximately 200 active proceedings; and managed workforce governance for industrial operations employing approximately 17,000 people. Its work also includes employer substitutions, REPSE and specialised-services compliance, labour and social-security inspections, and multi-state collective bargaining.\n\nThe practice is led by Eduardo Garduño, Javier Atzin Vallejo and Jaime Bustamante, with Associate-level contribution from José Alberto Díaz Méndez and other team members on significant employer mandates. This combination of collective labour capability, contentious experience, compliance work and multi-jurisdictional implementation warrants consideration for coverage.",
