        "b10": "DeForest’s Labour & Employment practice advises employers whose workforce stability is fundamental to business continuity. The team combines labour advisory, litigation, collective labour relations, social security, compliance and workforce governance for multinational and domestic companies operating throughout Mexico.\n\nThe practice supports the full employment lifecycle, from day-to-day advice and preventive documentation to collective bargaining, workforce restructurings, labour and social-security audits, inspections, constitutional proceedings and litigation portfolios. Its work is concentrated in labour-intensive and regulated sectors, including automotive and advanced manufacturing, infrastructure, energy, logistics, retail, technology, telecommunications and financial services.\n\nThe department is led by Eduardo Garduño, with Javier Atzin Vallejo and Jaime Bustamante as Partners. The wider team includes José Alberto Díaz Méndez, Andrés Cabrera Gómez, Raymundo Carreño, Edgar Barreto and Erick Pérez. Their combined experience supports collective labour strategy, compliance, social security, litigation and regional implementation.\n\nRepresentative work includes post-acquisition labour integration across six Mexican states for more than 5,000 employees; collective bargaining and union-representation matters involving strike-prevention strategy; nationwide litigation portfolios; workforce restructurings; and labour and social-security governance for industrial operations. The practice also manages approximately 700 labour disputes through its Puebla team and has assisted more than 20 clients in Ministry of Labor inspections during the last 12 months.",
        "c2": "DeForest requests consideration for coverage in Labour & Employment in Mexico. The practice presents employer-side work across the areas most central to workforce continuity in labour-intensive operations: collective bargaining and union-representation disputes, post-acquisition integration, workforce restructuring, employment litigation, constitutional labour procedure, labour inspections, social security and preventive governance.\n\nThe team has implemented a post-acquisition labour integration across six Mexican states for more than 5,000 employees; designed collective bargaining and union-representation strategies that prevented work stoppages; coordinated nationwide litigation portfolios involving approximately 200 active proceedings; and managed workforce governance for industrial operations employing approximately 17,000 people. Its work also includes employer substitutions, REPSE and specialised-services compliance, labour and social-security inspections, and multi-state collective bargaining.\n\nThe practice is led by Eduardo Garduño, Javier Atzin Vallejo and Jaime Bustamante, with Associate-level contribution from José Alberto Díaz Méndez and other team members on significant employer mandates. This combination of collective labour capability, contentious experience, compliance work and multi-jurisdictional implementation warrants consideration for coverage.",
