wing an automotive-sector acquisition across six Mexican states, standardising employment agreements, collective bargaining agreements, internal regulations, mixed commissions and governance policies for a workforce exceeding 5,000 employees, while related proceedings remain active. He also leads employment litigation and governance work for a major industrial employer in Puebla, with active proceedings involving aggregate exposure approaching MXN 280 million. His collective labour work includes negotiations that prevented a threatened strike in the automotive supply chain, as well as workforce transformation and collective bargaining work across manufacturing facilities in Puebla, Veracruz and Tlaxcala. Eduardo brings more than two decades of experience advising multinational employers and has senior public-sector experience.\nY\n\n\nJavier Atzin Vallejo\nSuggested ranking: Individual Band ranking\nJavier Atzin Vallejo is a Partner whose practice combines labour, social security and regulatory compliance work for industrial and infrastructure businesses. He leads collective labour and workforce-governance mandates, including a union-representation dispute at a manufacturing facility in Querétaro where the firm’s negotiation and litigation strategy prevented work stoppages and avoided escalation under the USMCA Rapid Response Labor Mechanism. He also leads labour governance for major energy infrastructure projects, covering project-specific employment structures, compliance, dispute prevention and workforce demobilisation; the work included more than 20 labour disputes, most resolved through conciliation. Javier has led constitutional labour proceedings resulting in recognition of a company representative’s standing and restoration of the client’s right to defend itself. His current work also includes employment, union and social-security structures for an industrial workforce of approximately 17,000 employees.\nY\n\n\nJaime Bustamante\nSuggested ranking: Individual Band ranking\nJaime Bustamante is a Partner advising employers on employment disputes, compliance investigations, labour inspections and workforce governance. He leads a coordinated litigation, constitutional-review and negotiation strategy for a technology and engineering business facing proceedings with potential exposure approaching MXN 16 million, including negotiations relating to two adverse judgments valued at approximately MXN 6.5 million. He also leads labour litigation, audit and inspection-readiness work for a financial-services business, where three Ministry of Labor and IMSS inspections concluded without fines despite combined potential exposure approaching MXN 2 million. Jaime has led a sensitive internal employment investigation involving allegations against a senior executive, coordinating evidence gathering, legal analysis and decision-making; the process prevented litigation valued at approximately MXN 750,000. Before joining DeForest, he served as Legal Director for Mexico, Central and South America at ManpowerGroup.\nY\n\n\nJosé Alberto Díaz Méndez\nSuggested ranking: Associate to Watch\nJosé Alberto Díaz Méndez is an Associate working on labour, social security and regulatory restructuring matters. He is named as matter lead on a multidisciplinary REPSE mandate involving review of a corporate structure, specialised-services model, bylaws and registration, together with an audit of more than 80 suppliers. He is also named on an employer-substitution process for a diversified group employing more than 1,200 people, where the legal strategy reactivated a cancelled employer registration and enabled the transition without disrupting operations. His work further includes a restructuring affecting approximately eight companies across multiple Mexican states, involving a specialised-services entity, employer substitutions, workforce transition and parallel social-security litigation concerning tax assessments at two manufacturing facilities.\nN\n\n\nErick Pérez\nErick Pérez is an Associate in DeForest’s Labour & Employment practice. He is listed as a team member on employer-side mandates involving active industrial litigation portfolios, nationwide employment proceedings and union-representation strategy.\nN\n\n\nAndrés Cabrera Gómez\nAndrés Cabrera Gómez is an Associate who leads the firm’s Labour & Employment practice in the Bajío region. His profile focuses on regional implementation and litigation coordination in jurisdictions adapting to the post-reform judicial system.\nN\n\n\nRaymundo Carreño\nRaymundo Carreño is an Associate with nearly four decades of experience at a confidential client, including service as General Legal Director. His background includes corporate labour relations, regulatory transitions, union negotiations and organisational restructurings in the automotive sector.\nN\n\n\nEdgar Barreto\nEdgar Barreto is an Associate with more than two decades of experience in labour litigation, social security and internal governance for large-scale employers. His profile includes IMSS processes, inspection frameworks, documentation harmonisation and preventive contingency management.\nN\n\n\n\nB10 What is this department best known for?\nPlease include: industry sector expertise; key types of work; areas of recent growth.\nAddress any feedback on our recent coverage of your department (500 word count limit)\nDeForest’s Labour & Employment practice advises employers whose workforce stability is fundamental to business continuity. The team combines labour advisory, litigation, collective labour relations, social security, compliance and workforce governance for multinational and domestic companies operating throughout Mexico.\n\nThe practice supports the full employment lifecycle, from day-to-day advice and preventive documentation to collective bargaining, workforce restructurings, labour and social-security audits, inspections, constitutional proceedings and litigation portfolios. Its work is concentrated in labour-intensive and regulated sectors, including automotive and advanced manufacturing, infrastructure, energy, logistics, retail, technology, telecommunications and financial services.\n\nThe department is led by Eduardo Garduño, with Javier Atzin Vallejo and Jaime Bustamante as Partners. The wider team includes José Alberto Díaz Méndez, Andrés Cabrera Gómez, Raymundo Carreño, Edgar Barreto and Erick Pérez. Their combined experience supports collective labour strategy, compliance, social security, litigation and regional implementation.\n\nRepresentative work includes post-acquisition labour integration across six Mexican states for more than 5,000 employees; collective bargaining and union-representation matters involving strike-prevention strategy; nationwide litigation portfolios; workforce restructurings; and labour and social-security governance for industrial operations. The practice also manages approximately 700 labour disputes through its Puebla team and has assisted more than 20 clients in Ministry of Labor inspections during the last 12 months.\n\nC1 If you have used barristers / advocates in the UK, Australia, Hong Kong, India, Malaysia, New Zealand or Sri Lanka please provide the information below (Optional)\nBarrister/advocate name\nFirm / Set\nComments\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nC2 Feedback on our coverage of this practice area (Optional)\nDeForest requests consideration for coverage in Labour & Employment in Mexico. The practice presents employer-side work across the areas most central to workforce continuity in labour-intensive operations: collective bargaining and union-representation disputes, post-acquisition integration, workforce restructuring, employment litigation, constitutional labour procedure, labour inspections, social security and preventive governance.\n\nThe team has implemented a post-acquisition labour integration across six Mexican states for more than 5,000 employees; designed collective bargaini